[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186161966,
      "id": "958cdcf3-aaba-5275-85f8-9531f10b3d0e",
      "title": "Using Column Operations to Create a Row of Zeros in a 4x4 Matrix"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "84d9f9b1-a3a1-5153-8439-40b189197889",
      ":question/problem": "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -10 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 5 & -9 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 7 & -2 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 16 & -26 \\\\ 9 & 14 & -44 & 52\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary column operations\n\n- $C_{2}:=\\ldots$\n- $C_{3}:=\\ldots$\n- $C_{4}:=C_{4}+5C_{1}$\n\neach of the form $C_i:=C_i+kC_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary column operations?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries in row 1 other than $a_{11}=-2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -10 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 5 & -9 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{C_{2}:=C_{2}+C_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & -10 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -9 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 14 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{C_{3}:=C_{3}-5C_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 7 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 16 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 14 & -44 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{C_{4}:=C_{4}+5C_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 7 & -2 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 16 & -26 \\\\ 9 & 14 & -44 & 52\\end{bmatrix}=B\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$C_{2}:=C_{2}+C_{1}$ and $C_{3}:=C_{3}-5C_{1}$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "f5426a3a-4321-55e9-84f5-c52a92950c25",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
