    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3b136c9c-7da4-5009-9538-816ffecf57de",
      ":question/problem": "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -5 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 5 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -8 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -9 & -8 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 25 & 8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -8 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -44 & -29 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary column operations\n\n- $C_{2}:=\\ldots$\n- $C_{3}:=\\ldots$\n- $C_{4}:=C_{4}-C_{1}$\n\neach of the form $C_i:=C_i+kC_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary column operations?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries in row 1 other than $a_{11}=-1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -5 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 5 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -8 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -9 & -8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{C_{2}:=C_{2}-5C_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 25 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -8 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -44 & -8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{C_{3}:=C_{3}-3C_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 25 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -8 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -44 & -29 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{C_{4}:=C_{4}-C_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 25 & 8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -8 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -44 & -29 & -3\\end{bmatrix}=B\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$C_{2}:=C_{2}-5C_{1}$ and $C_{3}:=C_{3}-3C_{1}$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
