    "question": {
      ":question/id": "61135b71-1aa2-53c6-a175-845d86995171",
      ":question/problem": "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 8 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 9 & -7 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -9 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -43 & 9 & -26 \\\\ 4 & -24 & 8 & -7 \\\\ 1 & -13 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary column operations\n\n- $C_{2}:=\\ldots$\n- $C_{3}:=\\ldots$\n- $C_{4}:=C_{4}-3C_{1}$\n\neach of the form $C_i:=C_i+kC_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary column operations?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries in row 1 other than $a_{11}=2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 8 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 9 & -7 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -9 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{C_{2}:=C_{2}-4C_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 9 & -43 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -24 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -13 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{C_{3}:=C_{3}+2C_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 9 & -43 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -24 & 8 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -13 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{C_{4}:=C_{4}-3C_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -43 & 9 & -26 \\\\ 4 & -24 & 8 & -7 \\\\ 1 & -13 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}=B\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$C_{2}:=C_{2}-4C_{1}$ and $C_{3}:=C_{3}+2C_{1}$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
